      {
        "id": "deck-rad-065",
        "deckId": "radiology",
        "deckName": "Oral Radiology (2017 Decks)",
        "category": "Radiation Physics",
        "front": "Identify the following parts of the dental x-ray tube (Items 11-18).[cite: 1]<br><br><img src='../images/dental x ray tube page 53.png' style='max-width: 100%; max-height: 180px; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 15px;'>",
        "answer": "See rationale for the numbered list.",
        "rationale": "<strong>11.</strong> Filament[cite: 1]<br><strong>12.</strong> Molybdenum cup[cite: 1]<br><strong>13.</strong> Electron stream[cite: 1]<br><strong>14.</strong> Tungsten target[cite: 1]<br><strong>15.</strong> Copper sleeve / Anode[cite: 1]<br><strong>16.</strong> Vacuum[cite: 1]<br><strong>17.</strong> X-ray beam[cite: 1]<br><strong>18.</strong> Leaded glass housing[cite: 1]<br><br>X-rays are generated when a stream of electrons (which are produced by the filament) travels from the cathode to the anode and is suddenly stopped by its impact on the tungsten target.[cite: 1] The filament located in the cathode is made of tungsten wire.[cite: 1] The small area on the target that the electrons strike is called the focal spot &rarr; it is the source of x-rays.[cite: 1]<br><br><strong>Notes:</strong><br>1. The size of the focal spot directly influences the x-ray definition &rarr; the larger the focal spot, the greater the loss of definition and sharpness of the image.[cite: 1]<br>2. Copper is used to house the anode because it is a good thermal conductor, dissipating heat from the tungsten target and reducing the risk of melting the target.[cite: 1]<br>3. The Molybdenum cup, also known as the electron focusing cup, electrostatically directs the electrons from the filament in the cathode to the focal spot on the anode.[cite: 1]",
        "type": "dental-deck"
      }
