        "front": "47. Which type of the following radiation poses the greatest danger to clinicians during a dental radiographic procedure?<br><br><div class=\"deck-options\">a. Alpha particles<br>b. Direct primary beam<br>c. Gamma<br>d. Secondary radiation and scatter</div>",
        "rawOptions": "a. Alpha particles<br>b. Direct primary beam<br>c. Gamma<br>d. Secondary radiation and scatter",
        "answer": "d. Secondary radiation and scatter",
        "rationale": "<strong>Operator Safety:</strong> While the primary beam is stronger, the clinician should never be in the path of the primary beam. The true everyday occupational hazard for the clinician standing 6 feet away is the secondary scatter radiation bouncing off the patient's facial tissues.",
