        "front": "67. Which factor accounts for the varying radiosensitivity of tissues?<br><br><div class=\"deck-options\">a. Rate of cell replication<br>b. Cellular differentiation<br>c. Repair and recovery rate<br>d. Location of chromosomes</div>",
        "rawOptions": "a. Rate of cell replication<br>b. Cellular differentiation<br>c. Repair and recovery rate<br>d. Location of chromosomes",
        "answer": "a. Rate of cell replication (and differentiation)",
        "rationale": "<strong>Law of Bergonie and Tribondeau:</strong> Tissues are most susceptible to radiation damage if they have a high rate of mitosis (cell division/replication) and remain highly undifferentiated. This is why bone marrow and reproductive cells are highly sensitive, while mature nerve cells are resistant.",
