        "deckName": "Day 1 Block 2: Pathology, Micro, Embryo & Histology",
        "category": "Pathology & Microbiology",
        "front": "4. Following crown formation, Hertwig's Epithelial Root Sheath (HERS) guides the formation of the roots. When HERS breaks down, it leaves behind microscopic clusters of epithelial cells within the periodontal ligament. These clusters are known as:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Rests of Serres</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Epithelial Rests of Malassez</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Enamel Pearls</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Tomes' Fibers</div></div>",
