        "deckName": "Day 1 Block 2: Pathology, Micro, Embryo & Histology",
        "category": "Pathology & Microbiology",
        "front": "23. In the microbiological testing for Syphilis, the causative agent *Treponema pallidum* cannot be cultured on standard agar plates and is too thin to be seen with a regular light microscope. Which microscopic technique is required to visualize this live spirochete?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Electron microscopy</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Dark-field microscopy</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Phase-contrast microscopy</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Fluorescence microscopy</div></div>",
