        "category": "Pathology & Microbiology",
        "front": "24. Localized Aggressive Periodontitis (now classified as Periodontitis Stage III/IV, Grade C) classically presents with rapid, severe vertical bone loss exclusively around the permanent first molars and incisors in otherwise healthy teenagers. Which specific pathogen is definitively linked to this disease?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Porphyromonas gingivalis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Tannerella forsythia</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans (Aa)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Fusobacterium nucleatum</div></div>",
