        "category": "Pathology & Microbiology",
        "front": "28. Tuberculosis (TB) is a highly infectious respiratory disease caused by *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*. Histologically, a biopsied TB lesion in the lungs or oral cavity is characterized by:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> A massive infiltrate of eosinophils and mast cells.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Caseating (cheese-like) necrosis surrounded by a granuloma of epithelioid macrophages and multinucleated giant cells.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Liquefactive necrosis filled with strictly polymorphonuclear neutrophils (PMNs).</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> A proliferation of dysplastic epithelial cells with keratin pearls.</div></div>",
