        "deckName": "Day 1 Block 2: Pathology, Micro, Embryo & Histology",
        "category": "Pathology & Microbiology",
        "front": "30. A patient presents with a deep, throbbing pain in tooth #46. Radiographs show a massive carious lesion into the pulp and a distinct, localized radiopacity (dense bone) sitting exactly at the apex of the mesial root. The tooth is non-vital. What is this radiographic finding called?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Periapical cemental dysplasia</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Condensing osteitis (Focal sclerosing osteomyelitis)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Cementoblastoma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Idiopathic osteosclerosis</div></div>",
