        "front": "31. A 5-year-old child presents with a high fever, malaise, and numerous painful, fluid-filled vesicles that have ruptured to form ulcers throughout their entire oral cavity, including the gingiva, tongue, and lips. The gums are swollen and bleeding. This is the classic first-time presentation of:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Herpangina</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Primary Herpetic Gingivostomatitis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Recurrent Aphthous Stomatitis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Varicella-Zoster infection (Chickenpox)</div></div>",
