        "front": "32. The Odontogenic Keratocyst (OKC) is notorious in oral surgery for its high recurrence rate. If a patient presents with multiple OKCs, multiple basal cell carcinomas on their skin, and bifid (split) ribs, they most likely have a genetic mutation (PTCH gene) known as:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Gardner Syndrome</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Peutz-Jeghers Syndrome</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Gorlin Syndrome (Nevoid Basal Cell Carcinoma Syndrome)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Treacher Collins Syndrome</div></div>",
