        "front": "12. In the design of a cast gold onlay, a \"bevel\" is frequently placed on the cavosurface margins. What is the primary restorative purpose of this bevel?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> To increase the bulk and strength of the gold margin.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> To remove unsupported enamel rods and allow the gold to be burnished (thinned out) for a perfect marginal seal.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> To create physical retention so the onlay does not fall out.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> To increase the surface area for the resin cement.</div></div>",
