        "category": "Restorative & Community",
        "front": "46. A patient presents with a fractured mesio-incisal angle of tooth #21. You plan to restore it with a Class IV composite. To perfectly blend the composite with the natural tooth and hide the transition line, you should:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Create a 90-degree butt joint margin on the facial enamel.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Place a long, infinite bevel on the facial enamel.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Use a macrofilled composite resin.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Do not etch the enamel.</div></div>",
