        "front": "50. When mixing a zinc phosphate cement for the permanent luting of a cast gold crown, the dentist must mix the powder into the liquid in very small increments over a wide area of a chilled glass slab. Why?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> To prevent the cement from freezing.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Because the acid-base reaction is highly exothermic (releases massive heat) and can cook the pulp if not dissipated.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> To incorporate air bubbles into the mix.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> To activate the photoinitiator.</div></div>",
