        "front": "64. When preparing a tooth for a composite restoration, dentists frequently place a 'bevel' on the enamel margins. Aside from aesthetics, what is the profound microscopic benefit of an enamel bevel?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> It decreases the C-factor.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> It exposes the ends of the enamel rods, vastly increasing the surface area and the strength of the acid-etched bond.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> It prevents the composite from shrinking.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It eliminates the need for a bonding agent.</div></div>",
