        "front": "31. An impression of a crown preparation is ruined because the gingival margins are completely obscured by blood and crevicular fluid. To stop the bleeding and physically push the gums away from the tooth, a retraction cord soaked in a hemostatic agent is used. Which common hemostatic agent operates by forming a bulky coagulum (clot) to plug the capillaries?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Epinephrine</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Ferric Sulfate or Aluminum Chloride</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Hydrogen Peroxide</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Sodium Hypochlorite</div></div>",
