        "category": "Prostho & Materials",
        "front": "33. What is the fundamental difference in the firing (setting) process between heat-cured acrylic (used for making final denture bases) and cold-cured/auto-cured acrylic (used for repairs or custom trays)?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Heat-cured acrylic uses camphorquinone as an initiator.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Heat-cured acrylic has more residual unreacted monomer and is highly porous.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Cold-cured acrylic requires a tertiary amine activator (like dimethyl-p-toluidine) to break down the benzoyl peroxide without heat.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Cold-cured acrylic shrinks 10x more.</div></div>",
