        "front": "44. A patient has an old, terribly fitting complete upper denture that rocks back and forth. You notice severe, folded, hyperplastic tissue growing over the borders of the denture flanges in the vestibule. This fibrous tissue overgrowth, caused by a sharp, overextended flange, is called:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Papillary hyperplasia</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Epulis Fissuratum (Inflammatory fibrous hyperplasia)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Peripheral ossifying fibroma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Ranula</div></div>",
