        "deckName": "Day 2 Block 2: Roentgenology, Diagnosis & Oral Surgery",
        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "15. What is the fundamental biomechanical principle of a dental elevator (e.g., a straight elevator) when used to luxate a tooth prior to using forceps?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> It acts strictly as a pulling mechanism.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> It acts as a lever and a wedge.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> It acts as a wheel and axle.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It cuts the periodontal ligament using a sharp blade.</div></div>",
