        "deckName": "Day 2 Block 2: Roentgenology, Diagnosis & Oral Surgery",
        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "17. A patient presents with a severe, spreading infection originating from a mandibular molar. The swelling is bilateral, involves the submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces, and physically elevates the patient's tongue against the roof of their mouth, threatening their airway. This life-threatening condition is:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Ludwig's Angina</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Actinomycosis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Pericoronitis</div></div>",
