        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "20. When prescribing antibiotics for a severe odontogenic infection, Penicillin VK is often the first choice. However, if the patient has a documented anaphylactic allergy to Penicillin, what is the standard alternative antibiotic prescribed in dentistry?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Amoxicillin</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Cephalexin (Keflex)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Clindamycin or Azithromycin</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Tetracycline</div></div>",
