        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "21. A patient presents with a suspicious, irregular, white, non-wipeable lesion on the lateral border of the tongue. You decide a biopsy is required. If you perform an 'Incisional Biopsy', what exactly are you doing?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Removing the entire lesion completely along with a margin of healthy tissue.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Removing only a small, representative slice of the lesion and a piece of adjacent normal tissue.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Using a needle to suck fluid out of the lesion.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Scraping the top surface of the lesion with a brush.</div></div>",
