        "front": "26. Which type of biopsy is strictly indicated for evaluating a suspected mucosal blister or blistering disease (like Pemphigus or Pemphigoid) so that the fragile blister roof is not destroyed during the procedure?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Fine Needle Aspiration (FNA)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Punch biopsy</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Incisional biopsy placed entirely within the blister</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Peri-lesional incisional biopsy (taking the edge of the blister and normal tissue)</div></div>",
