        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "46. Cone Beam Computed Tomography (CBCT) has revolutionized oral surgery and implantology. What is the fundamental difference between a CBCT scan and a traditional medical CT scan?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> CBCT provides 2D images, while medical CT provides 3D images.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> CBCT uses a cone-shaped x-ray beam and a flat-panel detector to capture the entire volume in a single rotation, resulting in vastly lower radiation.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> CBCT uses magnetic fields instead of x-rays.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> CBCT cannot view soft tissue at all.</div></div>",
