        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "77. A panoramic radiograph of an adult patient reveals an incredibly bizarre, multilocular radiolucency in the posterior mandible that appears exactly like the strings of a \"tennis racket\" or a \"honeycomb\". The lesion is benign but highly infiltrative. What is the diagnosis?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Odontoma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Odontogenic Myxoma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Dentigerous Cyst</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Cementoblastoma</div></div>",
