        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "86. An oral surgeon is performing a surgical extraction. The assistant is using a high-volume suction tip. What is the ONLY fluid the assistant should be actively suctioning from the socket during the drilling of bone?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Pure blood to keep the socket dry.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> The sterile saline irrigation fluid to prevent the bone from overheating while maintaining visibility.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Pus and necrotic tissue.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Saliva from the floor of the mouth.</div></div>",
