        "category": "Roentgenology & Surgery",
        "front": "89. During an examination, you notice a massive, red/purple, rubbery swelling on a child's lip. When you press firmly on it with a clear glass slide (diascopy), the lesion turns completely white (blanches). When you let go, it instantly fills with blood and turns red again. What is the diagnosis?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Amalgam tattoo</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Hemangioma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Squamous Cell Carcinoma</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Fibroma</div></div>",
