        "front": "6. A patient presents with a massively swollen, highly infected periapical abscess on tooth #30. You attempt an Inferior Alveolar Nerve Block (IANB), but the tooth will not get numb. What is the primary pharmacological reason for this failure?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> The infection increases the pH, destroying the anesthetic molecules.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> The acidic environment (low pH) of the infection forces the anesthetic to remain in its ionized (charged) form, preventing it from crossing the nerve membrane.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> The bacteria physically consume the lidocaine.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> The infection severs the nerve completely.</div></div>",
