        "front": "20. A patient in the recovery room after IV sedation is given a prescription for an opioid (e.g., Hydrocodone). The assistant notices the patient's breathing has dropped to 6 breaths per minute, their pupils are pinpoint (miosis), and they are unresponsive. What is the specific competitive antagonist (antidote) that MUST be given immediately to reverse the opioid overdose?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Flumazenil</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Naloxone (Narcan)</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Epinephrine</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Atropine</div></div>",
