        "front": "23. Penicillin is a bactericidal antibiotic that is considered the first-line treatment for most odontogenic infections. What is the specific mechanism by which Penicillin kills bacteria?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> It binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, halting protein synthesis.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> It inhibits the transpeptidase enzyme (Penicillin-Binding Proteins), completely preventing the cross-linking of the peptidoglycan cell wall.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> It destroys the bacterial DNA gyrase.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It pokes holes in the plasma membrane.</div></div>",
