        "front": "24. Macrolide antibiotics (like Erythromycin) and Tetracyclines are 'Bacteriostatic'. Penicillin is 'Bactericidal'. Why is it a terrible clinical error to prescribe a Bacteriostatic drug and a Bactericidal drug at the exact same time?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> They will react and form a toxic crystal in the blood.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> They cancel each other out; Penicillin requires actively dividing, growing bacteria to break their cell walls, but Bacteriostatic drugs put the bacteria to sleep, rendering the Penicillin totally useless.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> The combination guarantees an anaphylactic reaction.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It forces the bacteria to mutate instantly.</div></div>",
