        "category": "Anesthesia & Pharmacology",
        "front": "31. In conscious sedation, Nitrous Oxide ($N_2O$) is the safest and most common inhalation agent. The 'Blood-Gas Partition Coefficient' measures how easily an anesthetic dissolves in the blood. Nitrous Oxide has an incredibly LOW blood-gas partition coefficient (0.47). What does this mean clinically?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> It is highly potent and will put the patient in a deep coma.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> It barely dissolves in the blood at all, meaning it reaches the brain instantly for a very rapid onset, and leaves the body instantly for a very rapid recovery.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> It dissolves completely in the blood, taking hours to take effect.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It binds permanently to hemoglobin.</div></div>",
