        "front": "40. In pharmacokinetics, the \"First-Pass Effect\" is a crucial concept when administering oral medications (like Tylenol or Valium). What does the First-Pass effect describe?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> The drug goes straight to the brain first.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> The drug is absorbed by the stomach and immediately routed through the portal vein to the Liver, where a massive percentage of the drug is destroyed/metabolized before it ever reaches the systemic bloodstream.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> The drug passes through the kidneys unchanged.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> The first time a patient takes a drug, they are highly sensitive to it.</div></div>",
