        "front": "76. Why is the combination of Epinephrine with a non-selective Beta-blocker like Propranolol dangerous, whereas combining Epinephrine with a cardioselective Beta-1 blocker like Metoprolol is significantly safer?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Metoprolol destroys epinephrine on contact.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Metoprolol only blocks Beta-1 receptors in the heart, leaving the vascular Beta-2 receptors open to provide protective vasodilation and prevent unopposed alpha-1 vasoconstriction.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Propranolol is an ester, whereas Metoprolol is an amide.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Metoprolol causes severe hypotension when mixed with epinephrine.</div></div>",
