        "front": "87. The phenomenon known as 'Epinephrine Reversal' (Dale's vasomotor reversal) occurs when Epinephrine is administered to a patient who has previously received an Alpha-1 receptor blocker (like Prazosin). What happens to the patient's blood pressure?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Blood pressure shoots up dangerously high.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Blood pressure paradoxically drops (hypotension) because the alpha-1 vasoconstrictor action is blocked, leaving only beta-2 vasodilation.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Blood pressure remains completely unchanged.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Heart rate drops to zero.</div></div>",
