        "front": "8. A 4-year-old child presents with a massive carious exposure of the vital pulp on a primary mandibular second molar. The treatment of choice to preserve the vitality of the radicular pulp is a pulpotomy. Which medicament was historically the gold standard for fixing the pulpal stumps, though MTA and Ferric Sulfate are heavily replacing it today?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Calcium hydroxide</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Zinc oxide eugenol</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Formocresol</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Sodium hypochlorite</div></div>",
