        "front": "9. A 7-year-old child takes a baseball to the face, and tooth #8 (the permanent maxillary right central incisor) is completely avulsed (knocked out). The mother put the tooth in milk and arrived at the clinic 30 minutes later. The apex of the root is wide open (incompletely formed). What is the prognosis and treatment?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Replant the tooth immediately, splint flexibly for 1-2 weeks, and observe for revascularization.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Do not replant the tooth; place a pediatric partial denture.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Perform a root canal on the tooth in your hand, then replant it.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Scrape the root clean with a curette to remove dead cells before replanting.</div></div>",
