        "deckId": "day3_block1",
        "deckName": "Day 3 Block 1: Pediatric Dentistry & Orthodontics",
        "category": "Pediatric & Orthodontics",
        "front": "25. A mother is concerned because her 8-year-old child's new permanent central incisors have heavily serrated, bumpy ridges on the cutting edges. You reassure her this is perfectly normal and they will wear flat over time. These bumps are called:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Cingulums</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Mamelons</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Tubercles</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Enamel pearls</div></div>",
