        "front": "29. A 7-year-old child presents with a severe thumb-sucking habit. Clinically, what is the classic triad of malocclusion signs associated with severe, prolonged thumb-sucking?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Anterior open bite, flared maxillary incisors, and a narrow, V-shaped maxillary arch (often causing a posterior crossbite).</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Anterior deep bite, retroclined maxillary incisors, and a wide maxillary arch.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Skeletal Class III underbite and missing lateral incisors.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Impacted canines and generalized spacing.</div></div>",
