        "front": "32. In pediatric restorative dentistry, a Stainless Steel Crown (SSC) is the restoration of choice for a severely decayed primary molar. When preparing a tooth for an SSC, what is the absolute most critical geometric aspect of the preparation to ensure the crown snaps on and stays retained?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> A massive, deep shoulder margin at the gingiva.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Leaving the cervical bulge (undercut) intact on the buccal and lingual surfaces.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> A completely flat occlusal surface with 4mm of reduction.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Perfectly parallel axial walls.</div></div>",
