        "front": "40. In pediatric traumatology, an Ellis Class III fracture of an anterior tooth is clinically defined as:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> A fracture involving only the enamel.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> A fracture involving enamel and dentin, but the pulp is not exposed.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> A fracture involving enamel, dentin, and an actual exposure of the vital pulp.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> A fracture of the root below the gumline.</div></div>",
