        "deckId": "day3_block1",
        "deckName": "Day 3 Block 1: Pediatric Dentistry & Orthodontics",
        "category": "Pediatric & Orthodontics",
        "front": "41. You notice a pediatric patient has tiny, white, keratin-filled cysts scattered along the crest of the alveolar ridge of their gums. They are completely harmless and will shed on their own. These are remnants of the dental lamina and are called:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Epstein pearls</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Bohn's nodules</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Dental lamina cysts</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Mucocele</div></div>",
