        "category": "Jurisprudence & Ethics",
        "front": "50. A patient requests a copy of their dental records to take to a new dentist. You tell them it will cost P5,000 for the photocopying fee. Is this ethical?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Yes, records are expensive to maintain.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Yes, you own the records so you can charge whatever you want.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> No, dentists are ethically bound to furnish records or summaries either for free or for a nominal, reasonable administrative fee (the actual cost of copying).</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> No, you should never release records to anyone.</div></div>",
