        "category": "Jurisprudence & Ethics",
        "front": "51. A newly licensed dentist opens a clinic and forgets to pay their Professional Tax Receipt (PTR) because they 'didn't know it was required.' The city penalizes them. In legal terms, the doctrine 'Ignorantia juris non excusat' applies, which means:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> The law is harsh, but it is the law.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Ignorance of the law excuses no one from compliance therewith.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> The thing speaks for itself.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Let the buyer beware.</div></div>",
