        "category": "Jurisprudence & Ethics",
        "front": "62. You are extracting a tooth, and the patient suddenly screams, 'Ouch! You broke my jaw!' In a malpractice lawsuit, the patient's sudden, spontaneous exclamation made in the heat of the moment during the accident is admissible as evidence under the doctrine of:<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Res ipsa loquitur</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Res gestae</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Stare decisis</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Hearsay</div></div>",
