        "deckName": "Day 3 Block 3: Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "category": "Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "front": "27. In the classification of endodontic-periodontal lesions, what is a 'Primary Endodontic lesion with Secondary Periodontic involvement'?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Plaque travels down a deep gum pocket and infects the apex of the tooth.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> A dead pulp creates a massive apical abscess that drains UP the side of the root, destroying the PDL and creating a deep, narrow periodontal pocket.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> A fractured root causes the gums to recede.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> A tooth with two separate, unrelated diseases.</div></div>",
