        "deckId": "day3_block3",
        "deckName": "Day 3 Block 3: Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "category": "Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "front": "37. You are treating a patient with Necrotizing Ulcerative Gingivitis (NUG), famously known as 'Trench Mouth'. What is the classic, pathognomonic clinical presentation of NUG?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> Painless, pale, enlarged gums with no bleeding.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Blunted, 'punched-out' cratered interdental papillae covered by a gray pseudomembrane, accompanied by severe pain and extreme fetor oris (bad breath).</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Gingiva that strips away from the tooth perfectly intact.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Massive bone loss with totally healthy looking pink gums.</div></div>",
