        "category": "Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "front": "45. After periodontal scaling and root planing (SRP), the gums heal. How does the gingival tissue physically reattach to the freshly planed, clean root surface?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> By forming a new periodontal ligament.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> By growing new cementum.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> Via a Long Junctional Epithelium.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> It never attaches; a gap remains permanently.</div></div>",
