        "category": "Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "front": "48. During apical surgery (apicoectomy), the surgeon cuts off the bottom 3 mm of the root. Why is it absolutely mandatory to resect specifically the apical 3 mm?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> To make the tooth easier to extract later.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> Because the vast majority of apical deltas, microscopic accessory canals, and lateral branches that harbor trapped bacteria are located in the apical 3 mm.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> To avoid the maxillary sinus.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> Because the bur is exactly 3 mm wide.</div></div>",
