        "front": "54. A patient took a massive blow to their front teeth yesterday. You perform an Electric Pulp Test (EPT) on the traumatized incisor, and the result is negative (no response). What is the correct interpretation and treatment?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> The nerve is permanently dead; perform a root canal immediately.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> The EPT reading is likely a false negative due to 'pulp shock'; re-evaluate the tooth in 2 to 4 weeks before doing any treatment.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> The tooth is fractured and must be extracted.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> The machine is broken.</div></div>",
