        "deckName": "Day 3 Block 3: Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "category": "Periodontics & Endodontics",
        "front": "62. The radiographic apex of a tooth is what you see on an x-ray. However, the 'Major Apical Foramen' (the actual physical hole where the nerve exits the root) rarely exits exactly at the radiographic tip. On average, how far does the major foramen deviate from the radiographic apex?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> It does not deviate; it is exactly at the apex 100% of the time.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> 0.5 to 1.0 millimeters</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> 2.0 to 3.0 millimeters</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> 5.0 millimeters</div></div>",
