        "front": "88. In endodontic terminology, what is the fundamental difference between a Pulpotomy and a Pulpectomy?<br><br><div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 0.75rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>A.</strong> A pulpotomy removes the entire nerve; a pulpectomy removes only the top half.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>B.</strong> A pulpotomy is the removal of the coronal pulp chamber tissue only; a pulpectomy is the complete removal of all pulpal tissue from the chamber and the entire root canal system.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>C.</strong> A pulpotomy is used for dead teeth; a pulpectomy is used for live teeth.</div><div style=\"margin-bottom: 4px;\"><strong>D.</strong> They are the exact same procedure.</div></div>",
